Numeric Conformance
===================

WHAT THIS IS

Executable conformance programs, one per numeric concern. Each is a complete
Kairo program with a `main` that returns 0 when every check in it passes, or the
number of the FIRST check that failed. Nothing here is a lit test and nothing
here uses FileCheck; the assertion is the program's own arithmetic, so the same
file is a valid test the day stage 1 can emit a binary, with no harness to port.

    <kairo> Tests/NumericConformance/integer_32bit_band.k -o /tmp/t && /tmp/t
    echo $?          # 0 = pass, N = check N failed

Every check is numbered, and the number is its return code. A run that prints
`14` means check 14 failed and checks 1-13 passed; go to the comment above
check 14, which says what value was expected, why that value and not another,
and what a wrong answer implies about which part of the compiler is broken.


WHY THIS DIRECTORY EXISTS

A parser that mis-nests an expression produces a syntax error someone notices.
A compiler that mis-evaluates a number produces a program that runs, returns the
wrong answer, and blames the user. That failure mode is silent by construction,
so it has to be caught by assertion rather than by use.

The motivation is not hypothetical. The stage-0 compiler that builds stage 1 has
two confirmed numeric bugs, both silent, neither diagnosed, both shipped:

  1. THE 32-BIT BAND. An integer literal needing exactly 32 bits is typed
     *signed* 32-bit rather than promoted, so `0xFFFFFFFF` evaluates to `-1`.
     Every `v > 0xFFFFFFFF` range check against a 64-bit value is dead code, and
     every `v & 0xFFFFFFFF` mask is a no-op returning `v` unchanged.

  2. UNARY MINUS PRECEDENCE. Prefix `-` binds looser than a binary operator when
     folded: `-127 - 1` evaluates to `-126` and `-10 + 5` to `-15`. The wrong
     value propagates into runtime code.

Both were found by accident while chasing an unrelated parser bug. Neither would
have survived one assertion of the form "this expression has this value", which
is the entire content of this directory.

See Compiler/Support/Limits.k for the workarounds those bugs force on the
compiler's own sources, and for the rule that stops them being reintroduced.


RUNNING THEM

    ./run.sh /path/to/kairo

Runs every program and prints one line each. Exits non-zero if anything failed.
With no argument it uses whatever `kairo` is on PATH, which is stage 0.

Stage 0 compiles and runs these right now, and it FAILS five of them. That is
the point of recording its results: it proves the checks bite rather than
passing vacuously. Per-file detail is at the top of each file under
`STAGE 0 TODAY:`.

    bitwise_and_shifts.k                     ok
    conversions_and_narrowing.k              ok
    division_and_modulo.k                    ok
    float_basics.k                           FAIL  check 13
    float_exponent_notation.k                FAIL  check 3
    integer_128bit_range.k                   ok
    integer_32bit_band.k                     FAIL  check 3
    integer_literal_losslessness.k           skip  (see below)
    integer_width_boundaries.k               FAIL  check 9
    overflow_and_wraparound.k                ok
    unary_minus_precedence.k                 FAIL  check 1

Those five failures are four distinct stage-0 bugs, not four opinions:

    integer_32bit_band.k  }  the 32-bit band, bug 1 above. width_boundaries
    integer_width_...k    }  fails at the same bug one check earlier.
    unary_minus_...k         unary minus precedence, bug 2 above.
    float_exponent_...k      an exponent-form float literal with no `.` reads
                             as 0 (`1e-16`, `5e-1`), or crashes the produced
                             binary (`1e16`). With a fractional part it is
                             correct. Stage 1's lexer already handles all of
                             these correctly -- verified, this file is here to
                             hold that in place.
    float_basics.k           a narrowing cast to f32 is a no-op; f32 is
                             silently f64.

DO NOT "fix" a file to make stage 0 pass. Those five files are correct and
stage 0 is wrong.

When stage 1 can emit a binary, run all of them against it. Any file stage 0
passes and stage 1 fails is a stage-1 regression. Any file both fail is a bug
stage 1 inherited. The stage-0 column is a baseline, never a target -- the goal
is all ok, which stage 0 cannot reach.

integer_literal_losslessness.k is skipped under stage 0, not failed. Stage 0
rejects any literal at or above 2^64 outright, which is correct for stage 0's
own 64-bit world and wrong for Kairo's. It is a stage-1 test; run.sh recognises
the `REQUIRES: literals-above-64-bit` marker in its header and reports skip.

THERE IS NO "LITERAL TOO LARGE" DIAGNOSTIC, AND THERE SHOULD NOT BE ONE. Kairo
has fixed widths from 8 to 512 bits and arbitrary-precision bigints, so whether
a literal fits depends on the type it is used at: 2^200 is an error at i64, fine
at i256, and always fine at a bigint. The lexer sees the digits before any of
that is decided, so any bound it enforces is a guess. Its contract is capture,
not judgement -- escalate 64 -> 128 -> BigInt and hand sema something it can
range-check. Range enforcement belongs to whoever assigns the type, which means
sema, once literal typing exists. Do not add the check to the lexer.

These are not lit tests and lit does not collect them -- see lit.local.cfg.


WHAT DOES NOT WORK UNDER STAGE 1 YET

Nothing here executes under stage 1 today; there is no codegen. Two further gaps
are worth knowing before you try, so a parser limitation is not mistaken for a
broken test:

  - stage 1 does not parse `static_cast::<T>(...)` yet, for ANY T, including
    u64. Several files here use it where a narrowing or a reinterpretation is
    the thing being tested and there is no other way to write it, so they will
    not parse until that lands.
  - integer_literal_losslessness.k was written without `static_cast` on purpose
    and parses cleanly under stage 1 right now. It is the one file that can be
    checked end to end the moment codegen exists.

Until then the lexer can be checked directly with --print-tokens, which is where
both stage-0 literal bugs and the stage-1 escalation bug were actually found.


WHEN A CHECK FAILS AND YOU THINK THE CHECK IS WRONG

Some checks encode a language decision rather than a mathematical fact -- the
sign of `%` on negative operands, whether signed overflow wraps or traps,
whether `>>` on a signed value is arithmetic. Those are marked `DECISION:` in
the file. If one fails, the question is not "which value is correct" but "which
rule did Kairo choose"; fix the check to match the decision and write the
decision down somewhere it can be cited. Everything not marked `DECISION:` is
arithmetic and the check is right.


CONVENTIONS

  - `fn main() -> i32`, returns 0 or the failing check number. Check numbers are
    unique per file and never renumbered, so an old failure report stays
    readable; add new checks at the end.
  - Comparisons are written `if <actual> != <expected> { return N; }` so the
    check reads in the same direction as the comment above it.
  - Boolean checks are written `if (<expr>) != true { return N; }` rather than
    with `!`, because stage 0 parses `!a && !b` as `!(a && !b)`; avoiding `!`
    entirely keeps these files honest under both compilers.
  - Values that cannot be spelled as a literal without triggering bug 1 or 2 are
    built from small literals in-file, and the comment says why.
